To achieve the layout where an image and a paragraph are on the same line and the paragraph text wraps around the image, you can use CSS Flexbox. However, Flexbox is not the ideal tool for text wrapping around an image. Instead, you can use the `float` property or CSS Grid for this purpose. Below are examples for both methods:

### Using `float` Property

The `float` property is more straightforward for this kind of layout.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Image and Text Layout</title>
    <style>
        .container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
        }
        .image {
            float: left;
            margin-right: 20px;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        .text {
            overflow: hidden;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <img src="your-image.jpg" alt="Description" class="image" style="width: 150px;">
        <p class="text">
            Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum.
        </p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Using CSS Grid

If you prefer to use CSS Grid, you can create a grid layout and use the `grid-template-areas` property to define the layout.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Image and Text Layout</title>
    <style>
        .container {
            max-width: 600px;
            margin: 0 auto;
            padding: 20px;
            display: grid;
            grid-template-areas: 
                "image text"
                "image text";
            grid-gap: 20px;
        }
        .image {
            grid-area: image;
            width: 150px;
        }
        .text {
            grid-area: text;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="container">
        <img src="your-image.jpg" alt="Description" class="image">
        <p class="text">
            Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut labore et dolore magna aliqua. Ut enim ad minim veniam, quis nostrud exercitation ullamco laboris nisi ut aliquip ex ea commodo consequat. Duis aute irure dolor in reprehenderit in voluptate velit esse cillum dolore eu fugiat nulla pariatur. Excepteur sint occaecat cupidatat non proident, sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum.
        </p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation

1. **Using `float` Property:**
   - The `float: left;` on the image causes it to float to the left of the container.
   - The `overflow: hidden;` on the text ensures that the text wraps around the floated image.

2. **Using CSS Grid:**
   - The `grid-template-areas` property defines the layout areas for the image and text.
   - The `grid-gap` property adds space between the grid items.

Both methods will achieve the desired layout, but the `float` property is more commonly used for this specific scenario.

To align one button to the left and the other to the right within a parent container using Flexbox, you can use the `justify-content` property with the value `space-between`. This will distribute the space between the buttons, pushing one to the left and the other to the right.

Here’s an example:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
