Creating a Vitis HLS (High-Level Synthesis) implementation of a neural network for the MNIST handwritten digit recognition task involves several steps. Below is a simplified example of how you might implement a basic feedforward neural network using Vitis HLS. This example will include the key components of the network: input layer, hidden layer, and output layer.

### Prerequisites
- Vitis HLS environment set up
- Basic understanding of C++ and neural networks

### Step 1: Define the Network Structure
We will define a simple neural network with one hidden layer. The input layer will have 784 neurons (28x28 pixels), the hidden layer will have 128 neurons, and the output layer will have 10 neurons (one for each digit).

### Step 2: Define the Activation Function
For simplicity, we will use the ReLU activation function for the hidden layer and the softmax function for the output layer.

### Step 3: Implement the Network in C++
Here is a simplified version of the code:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <ap_fixed.h>
#include <ap_int.h>
#include <hls_math.h>

// Define fixed-point types
typedef ap_fixed<16, 6> weight_t;
typedef ap_fixed<16, 6> bias_t;
typedef ap_fixed<16, 6> data_t;

// ReLU activation function
data_t relu(data_t x) {
    return (x > 0) ? x : 0;
}

// Softmax function
void softmax(data_t input[10], data_t output[10]) {
    data_t max_val = input[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < 10; i++) {
        if (input[i] > max_val) {
            max_val = input[i];
        }
    }

    data_t sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        output[i] = hls::exp(input[i] - max_val);
        sum += output[i];
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        output[i] /= sum;
    }
}

// Forward pass of the neural network
void forward_pass(data_t input[784], data_t output[10], weight_t weights1[128][784], bias_t biases1[128], weight_t weights2[10][128], bias_t biases2[10]) {
    // Hidden layer
    data_t hidden[128];
    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
        hidden[i] = biases1[i];
        for (int j = 0; j < 784; j++) {
            hidden[i] += weights1[i][j] * input[j];
        }
        hidden[i] = relu(hidden[i]);
    }

    // Output layer
    data_t output_temp[10];
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        output_temp[i] = biases2[i];
        for (int j = 0; j < 128; j++) {
            output_temp[i] += weights2[i][j] * hidden[j];
        }
    }

    // Apply softmax
    softmax(output_temp, output);
}

// Top-level function for synthesis
void mnist_nn(data_t input[784], data_t output[10]) {
    // Initialize weights and biases (these should be pre-trained)
    weight_t weights1[128][784];
    bias_t biases1[128];
    weight_t weights2[10][128];
    bias_t biases2[10];

    // Load pre-trained weights and biases
    // For demonstration, we will initialize with dummy values
    for (int i = 0; i < 128; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 784; j++) {
            weights1[i][j] = 0.01;
        }
        biases1[i] = 0.01;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        for (int j = 0; j < 128; j++) {
            weights2[i][j] = 0.01;
        }
        biases2[i] = 0.01;
    }

    // Perform the forward pass
    forward_pass(input, output, weights1, biases1, weights2, biases2);
}
```

### Step 4: Synthesize the Design
To synthesize this design using Vitis HLS, you can use the following commands in the Vitis HLS environment:

```sh
vitis_hls
open_solution "solution1"
set_part {xc7z020clg400-1}
create_clock -period 10 -name default
csynth_design
export_design -format ip_catalog
```

### Step 5: Test the Design
You can test the design using a C++ testbench. Here is an example testbench:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include "mnist_nn.h"

int main() {
    data_t input[784];
    data_t output[10];

    // Initialize input with dummy values
    for (int i = 0; i < 784; i++) {
        input[i] = 0.1;
    }

    // Call the neural network function
    mnist_nn(input, output);

    // Print the output
    for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
        std::cout << "Output[" << i << "]: " << output[i] << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

### Step 6: Compile and Run the Testbench
You can compile and run the testbench using a C++ compiler:

```sh
g++ -I$XILINX_VIVADO/include -o testbench testbench.cpp
./testbench
```

This is a simplified example to get you started. In a real-world scenario, you would need to load pre-trained weights and biases, handle data normalization, and possibly optimize the design for better performance and resource utilization.

